  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-10 mb-4">1. Battery Chemistries: LFP vs. NMC Explained</h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Not all EV batteries are built the same. The automotive industry is primarily powered by two distinct cathode chemistries:
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8 not-prose">
    <div class="p-6 rounded-3xl bg-emerald-50/70 border-2 border-emerald-300">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
        <strong class="text-base font-black text-emerald-950">Lithium Iron Phosphate (LFP)</strong>
        <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-[10px] font-black bg-emerald-200 text-emerald-900">Highest Durability</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-emerald-800 font-medium leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Found in standard-range Tesla Model 3/Y, BYD Blade Battery, and modern electric motorbikes. Uses no cobalt or nickel.
      </p>
      <ul class="text-xs space-y-1.5 font-bold text-emerald-950">
        <li>• <strong>Cycle Life:</strong> 2,000 to 3,500 full charge cycles</li>
        <li>• <strong>Expected Lifespan:</strong> 350,000 to 500,000+ km (15+ years)</li>
        <li>• <strong>Charge Behavior:</strong> Can be safely charged to 100% regularly</li>
        <li>• <strong>Thermal Stability:</strong> Extremely high resistance to thermal runaway</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="p-6 rounded-3xl bg-blue-50/70 border-2 border-blue-300">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
        <strong class="text-base font-black text-blue-950">Nickel Manganese Cobalt (NMC)</strong>
        <span class="px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-[10px] font-black bg-blue-200 text-blue-900">High Energy Density</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-blue-800 font-medium leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Found in long-range luxury sedans, performance sports cars, and heavy electric SUVs. Maximizes driving range per kilogram.
      </p>
      <ul class="text-xs space-y-1.5 font-bold text-blue-950">
        <li>• <strong>Cycle Life:</strong> 1,200 to 1,800 full charge cycles</li>
        <li>• <strong>Expected Lifespan:</strong> 250,000 to 350,000 km (10-12 years)</li>
        <li>• <strong>Charge Behavior:</strong> Best capped at 80% for daily commuting</li>
        <li>• <strong>Cold Performance:</strong> Retains higher voltage in sub-zero climates</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-10 mb-4">2. Real-World Degradation: How Much Range Do You Lose?</h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Empirical field studies tracking over 100,000 commercial and private electric vehicles indicate that the average modern EV battery loses approximately <strong>1.0% to 1.5% of its total capacity per year</strong>.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Degradation is non-linear: a small initial drop of 2% to 3% typically occurs during the first 15,000 km as the solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) stabilizes on the anode. Following this initial settling period, degradation flattens into a very slow, predictable curve.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    After <strong>8 years and 160,000 kilometers</strong> of real-world daily driving, most EV battery packs still retain between <strong>85% to 90% of their original factory capacity</strong>. This is far above the standard 70% threshold that manufacturers use to trigger warranty replacements.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-10 mb-4">3. Four Golden Rules to Maximize Battery Longevity</h2>
  
  <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
    <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200">
      <strong class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950 block mb-1">1. Respect the 20% - 80% State of Charge (SoC) Rule for NMC Packs</strong>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Lithium-ion cells experience the highest mechanical and chemical stress when kept sitting at 100% full or depleted near 0%. For NMC vehicles, set your daily charging limit to 80% and reserve 100% charging for the night before a long highway road trip. (Note: LFP batteries can be charged to 100% weekly for cell balancing).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200">
      <strong class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950 block mb-1">2. Prioritize Overnight Level 2 AC Home Charging</strong>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Slow overnight AC charging (7 kW to 11 kW) produces minimal internal heat, preserving cell chemistry. Commercial DC fast chargers (150 kW - 350 kW) push enormous current into the pack, generating heat and localized lithium plating if overused daily.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200">
      <strong class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950 block mb-1">3. Avoid Storing Your Vehicle in Extreme Ambient Heat</strong>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Ambient temperatures exceeding 40°C (104°F) accelerate chemical aging. When possible, park in shaded garages or leave your EV plugged in so the active thermal management system can draw wall power to circulate cooling liquid through the battery pack.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200">
      <strong class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950 block mb-1">4. Never Leave an EV Parked at 0% for Extended Periods</strong>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Letting a battery sit completely empty for weeks can cause copper dendrite growth and severe voltage drop across individual cell groups. If going away on vacation, leave your battery charged between 50% and 60%.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-10 mb-4">4. What Will Battery Replacement Cost in 2028-2030?</h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    According to BloombergNEF and the International Energy Agency, battery pack costs have plunged from over <strong>$1,100 per kWh in 2010</strong> to approximately <strong>$115-$130 per kWh in 2026</strong>. By 2028-2030, next-generation sodium-ion and scaled LFP manufacturing is projected to push cell costs below <strong>$70 per kWh</strong>.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    By the time a current 2026 EV reaches 10 to 12 years of age, a replacement battery module will cost a fraction of today's price—comparable to the cost of replacing a blown head gasket or transmission in an aging luxury petrol car.
  </p>

